592	## Configuration Management
593	
594	### ⚠️ Important Security Warnings
595	
596	> **For comprehensive security information and vulnerability reporting**: See [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md)
597	
598	1. **Known security limitations**: Directory restrictions and command blocking can be bypassed through various methods including symlinks, command substitution, and absolute paths or code execution
599	
600	2. **Always change configuration in a separate chat window** from where you're doing your actual work. Claude may sometimes attempt to modify configuration settings (like `allowedDirectories`) if it encounters filesystem access restrictions.
601	
602	3. **The `allowedDirectories` setting currently only restricts filesystem operations**, not terminal commands. Terminal commands can still access files outside allowed directories.
603	
604	4. **For production security**: Use the [Docker installation](#option-6-docker-installation-🐳-⭐-auto-updates-no-nodejs-required) which provides complete isolation from your host system.
605	
606	### Configuration Tools
607	
608	You can manage server configuration using the provided tools:
609	
610	```javascript
611	// Get the entire config
612	get_config({})
613	
614	// Set a specific config value
615	set_config_value({ "key": "defaultShell", "value": "/bin/zsh" })
616	
617	// Set multiple config values using separate calls
618	set_config_value({ "key": "defaultShell", "value": "/bin/bash" })
619	set_config_value({ "key": "allowedDirectories", "value": ["/Users/username/projects"] })
620	```
621	
622	The configuration is saved to `config.json` in the server's working directory and persists between server restarts.
623	
624	#### Understanding fileWriteLineLimit
625	
626	The `fileWriteLineLimit` setting controls how many lines can be written in a single `write_file` operation (default: 50 lines). This limit exists for several important reasons:
627	
628	**Why the limit exists:**
629	- **AIs are wasteful with tokens**: Instead of doing two small edits in a file, AIs may decide to rewrite the whole thing. We're trying to force AIs to do things in smaller changes as it saves time and tokens
630	- **Claude UX message limits**: There are limits within one message and hitting "Continue" does not really work. What we're trying here is to make AI work in smaller chunks so when you hit that limit, multiple chunks have succeeded and that work is not lost - it just needs to restart from the last chunk
631	
632	**Setting the limit:**
633	```javascript
634	// You can set it to thousands if you want
635	set_config_value({ "key": "fileWriteLineLimit", "value": 1000 })
636	
637	// Or keep it smaller to force more efficient behavior
638	set_config_value({ "key": "fileWriteLineLimit", "value": 25 })
639	```
640	
641	**Maximum value**: You can set it to thousands if you want - there's no technical restriction.
642	
643	**Best practices**:
644	- Keep the default (50) to encourage efficient AI behavior and avoid token waste
645	- The system automatically suggests chunking when limits are exceeded
646	- Smaller chunks mean less work lost when Claude hits message limits
647	
648	### Best Practices
649	
650	1. **Create a dedicated chat for configuration changes**: Make all your config changes in one chat, then start a new chat for your actual work.
651	
652	2. **Be careful with empty `allowedDirectories`**: Setting this to an empty array (`[]`) grants access to your entire filesystem for file operations.
653	
654	3. **Use specific paths**: Instead of using broad paths like `/`, specify exact directories you want to access.
655	
656	4. **Always verify configuration after changes**: Use `get_config({})` to confirm your changes were applied correctly.
657	
658	## Command Line Options
659	
660	Desktop Commander supports several command line options for customizing behavior:
661	
662	### Disable Onboarding
663	
664	By default, Desktop Commander shows helpful onboarding prompts to new users (those with fewer than 10 tool calls). You can disable this behavior:
665	
666	```bash
667	# Disable onboarding for this session
668	node dist/index.js --no-onboarding
669	
670	# Or if using npm scripts
671	npm run start:no-onboarding
672	
673	# For npx installations, modify your claude_desktop_config.json:
674	{
675	  "mcpServers": {
676	    "desktop-commander": {
677	      "command": "npx",
678	      "args": [
679	        "-y",
680	        "@wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest",
681	        "--no-onboarding"
682	      ]
683	    }
684	  }
685	}
686	```
687	
688	**When onboarding is automatically disabled:**
689	- When the MCP client name is set to "desktop-commander"
690	- When using the `--no-onboarding` flag
691	- After users have used onboarding prompts or made 10+ tool calls
692	
693	**Debug information:**
694	The server will log when onboarding is disabled: `"Onboarding disabled via --no-onboarding flag"`
695	
696	## Using Different Shells
697	
698	You can specify which shell to use for command execution:
699	
700	```javascript
701	// Using default shell (bash or system default)
702	execute_command({ "command": "echo $SHELL" })
703	
704	// Using zsh specifically
705	execute_command({ "command": "echo $SHELL", "shell": "/bin/zsh" })
706	
707	// Using bash specifically
708	execute_command({ "command": "echo $SHELL", "shell": "/bin/bash" })
709	```
710	
711	This allows you to use shell-specific features or maintain consistent environments across commands.
712	
713	1. `execute_command` returns after timeout with initial output
714	2. Command continues in background
715	3. Use `read_output` with PID to get new output
716	4. Use `force_terminate` to stop if needed
717	
718	## Debugging
719	
720	If you need to debug the server, you can install it in debug mode:
721	
722	```bash
723	# Using npx
724	npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest setup --debug
725	
726	# Or if installed locally
727	npm run setup:debug
728	```
729	
730	This will:
731	1. Configure Claude to use a separate "desktop-commander" server
732	2. Enable Node.js inspector protocol with `--inspect-brk=9229` flag
733	3. Pause execution at the start until a debugger connects
734	4. Enable additional debugging environment variables
735	
736	To connect a debugger:
737	- In Chrome, visit `chrome://inspect` and look for the Node.js instance
738	- In VS Code, use the "Attach to Node Process" debug configuration
739	- Other IDEs/tools may have similar "attach" options for Node.js debugging
740	
741	Important debugging notes:
742	- The server will pause on startup until a debugger connects (due to the `--inspect-brk` flag)
743	- If you don't see activity during debugging, ensure you're connected to the correct Node.js process
744	- Multiple Node processes may be running; connect to the one on port 9229
745	- The debug server is identified as "desktop-commander-debug" in Claude's MCP server list
746	
747	Troubleshooting:
748	- If Claude times out while trying to use the debug server, your debugger might not be properly connected
749	- When properly connected, the process will continue execution after hitting the first breakpoint
750	- You can add additional breakpoints in your IDE once connected
